        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein to Eat While Cutting
          </h2>

          <p>
            The official RDA of 0.8 g of protein per kilogram of body weight is a floor set to prevent deficiency in sedentary adults, not a target for someone dieting and lifting. The International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g/kg per day for most exercising people, and a widely cited 2018 meta-analysis by Morton and colleagues found that the benefit of protein for muscle gains plateaued around 1.6 g/kg, with the upper end of its confidence interval near 2.2 g/kg. Those figures come mostly from people eating enough calories to maintain or gain.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a deficit, the case for eating toward the higher end gets stronger. Reviews of dieting lifters, including work by Helms and colleagues on lean athletes, have suggested intakes well above maintenance levels when the deficit is aggressive or the person is already lean. A frequently cited 2016 trial from McMaster University by Longland and colleagues put young men on a steep calorie deficit with hard training and compared a lower-protein diet with one providing about 2.4 g/kg; the higher-protein group held or gained lean mass and lost more fat over the four weeks. One short study is not a law, but it fits the broader pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical range for most people trying to lose fat while keeping muscle is therefore roughly 1.6 to 2.4 g/kg of body weight per day, toward the top if you are lean, training hard or cutting quickly, and toward the bottom if you have more weight to lose. People with a lot of body fat can base the number on a goal weight or estimated lean mass rather than current weight, which keeps the target realistic. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> explains the reasoning by goal, and the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> turns your weight into a daily number in seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spread that total across three or four meals of roughly 0.4 g/kg, or about 20 to 40 g each, rather than loading it into dinner. A scoop of powder fills the gap at the meal that usually comes up short, which for most people is breakfast or the afternoon. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or take medications that affect fluid or protein handling, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before raising your intake; for healthy kidneys, higher-protein diets in this range have not been shown to cause harm, but individual medical advice beats a general guide.
          </p>
        </div>

