          <p>
            Two traps show up again and again. The first is sugar. Some flavored powders, particularly those sold as everyday or family products, carry several grams of sugar per scoop, and some plant powders add cane sugar to mask earthy flavors. Two or three grams is negligible; ten or more is worth avoiding when every calorie counts. The second trap is the serving size itself. A label listing 30 g of protein per serving may be describing two heaping scoops and 170 calories, so compare products per 25 g of protein, not per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you mix it with matters as much as the powder. A 110-calorie isolate stirred into water stays at 110 calories. The same scoop in a cup of whole milk climbs to about 260, in a smoothie with banana, oats and nut butter it can reach 500 or more, and a coffee-shop style shake with syrup goes higher still. None of that is wrong if it replaces a meal and fits your plan, but it explains why people sometimes gain weight while adding a protein shake that was meant to help them lose it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixability and flavor matter more on a diet than anywhere else, because you are relying on the shake to feel like a treat when you are hungry. Lightly sweetened isolates and clear proteins mixed with cold water tend to be the lowest-calorie way to get a satisfying drink. Unflavored powders let you add protein to foods you were already eating, such as Greek yogurt, oatmeal or soup, without adding sweetness or many calories.
          </p>
